08/06/99

   o ZMP - Implemented MAXBUNDLESIZE.  This works the same as MAXPKTSIZE;
       For each network ("NODE" entry), place MAXBUNDLESIZE under the list
       of AREAs.  It's format is:

       MAXBUNDLESIZE <size in bytes> <address> <address> ...

       This restricts the size of Archived packets (known as bundles) to the
       given size for each of the given addresses.  You can use wildcards with
       the addresses.  You can use as many MAXBUNDLESIZE's as you want for
       different <size in bytes> values.

       The point of these functions is to enable people getting mail from
       you to import it more efficiently: for example, they might not have
       enough memory to import a single 2MB bundle which depacks to 6MB,
       taking all their memory.  So for their system you limit packet
       sizes to 256000 bytes and bundles to 512000 bytes, leaving them
       plenty of memory to work with.

       For example:

           ; Limit all packets to half a MB.
           MAXPKTSIZE     524288 *:*/*.*

           ; Limit all bundles to 1MB.  Packets are compressed, so about 6 or
           ; 8 512KB packets fit one of these.
           MAXBUNDLESIZE  1048576 *:*/*.*
           ; Limit these point's bundles to 512KB since they have just 4MB
           ; RAM to work in.
           MAXBUNDLESIZE  524288 2:442/107.3 2:442/107.1

       MAXBUNDLESIZE only really makes sense if you also have MAXPKTSIZE
       defined, and MAXPKTSIZE should be half or less than MAXBUNDLESIZE.
       Also, don't set them to very small values when the downlink(s) take
       lots of mail, otherwise your outbound will be filled with *lots* of
       files!

09/06/99

   o ZMP - Small change: cleaned up the shell output of the %RESCAN AreaFix
       command.  It shouldn't flicker now, plus it reports the area
       currently being rescanned.

10/06/99

   o ZMP - Previously, ZMP would create a bundle in outbound and then ALL
       packets added afterward while that first bundle was still there would
       be placed in bundles with /that/ days extension - e.g., if the first
       bundle was create on Wednesday, you'd only see .WE? bundles for that
       address.
       Now, ZMP will only work on bundles with the current day extension; i.e.
       after Wednesday ZMP will create .TH? bundles.
       This may become switchable.
   o ZMP - I've rewritten the outbound archive management many times, but I
       think I've now got it right - and if this isn't the most powerful
       outbound packet/archive management ever I'll be very suprised! The only
       problem is that if a downlink has MAXBUNDLESIZE set, and all possible
       bundles for the day (e.g. .MO[0-9]) are at max size, the packets added
       will no longer be in chronological order MO->WE->FR->SU, since ZMP
       attempts to keep bundle sizes to a minimum even if they do go over the
       max size. However, if a point leaves their mail for so long that this
       happens, they deserve it! ;-)

